=20
> Due to limitations of the Da Vince package, it does this function the "ol=
d"=20
> way that was limited to a few select fonts as originally determined  by=
=20
> Adobe, and then to make matters worse only one [Courier] actually=20
> completely functions.  There hasn't been any work on this package for wel=
l=20
> over a decade and it is shareware and not freeware.  Due to the nature of=
=20
> color backgraounds, it is not effective to use normal font quality thus t=
he=20
> equally functional BOLD is used.  I can demonstrate both to anyone; I thi=
nk=20
> virtually all will agree on this point.  Regardless, I can document how t=
o=20
> change that as well as the green-bar parameters [which includes various=
=20
> sub-areas with slightly different colors.  I set these colors by=20
> calibrating against good photos of actual green-bar 11 x 17 lineprinter=
=20
> paper, etc.  All of these settings are set by minor changes within a .ini=
=20
> file taken from their package.  I will document what I did and any user c=
an=20
> make appropriate modifications as they wish.  [And since this is sharewar=
e,=20
> I cannot police what users do regarding that.  However, I get the=20
> impression few if any actually do purchase this package these days; there=
=20
> are MANY bugs in the overall package and this is just a module I use in=
=20
> PEPS to bypass all the buggy and clumsy code that comes into play when yo=
u=20
> use it as intended by the author[s].  By integrating just this key file=
=20
> [and customized .imi file] we get what we need; The user can decide how=
=20
> much a nearly $100 package is worth to them given the poor overall qualit=
y=20
> [and there are many other components, many of them just very broken.  I g=
et=20
> the impression they just gave up on it these days, etc.]
>
> 2)  The second has to now be on hold until I can investigate this=20
> further.  It apparently uses a more flexible method that involves not onl=
y=20
> a variant of whee the first comes from regarding fonts, but more usefully=
,=20
> it can work with any font installed into the windows system overall and y=
ou=20
> specify the exact one you want.  Not every font works, but I have been=20
> getting good results with the Consolas font which is pretty good for the=
=20
> purpose. That is, until I found a problem:
>
> The lineprinter output on PDP-8 devices is defined to be exactly 132=20
> columns as would be created on 11 x 17 lineprinter paper and 66 lines=20
> high.  The PDP-8 software completely manages the output within these=20
> requirements.  However, this add-on has a limitation within it.  If a pag=
e=20
> happens to have lines wider than 120 columns, an extraneous CR/LF is=20
> performed which is just plain wrong [and throws off the rest of the page]=
.
>
> A good example of a very real PDP-8 program that does depend on this is=
=20
> the P?S/8 PAL assemble
>
=E2=80=8Br
>
  When the "/P" command-line switch is set, the norrow output of the=20
> console is increased to the presumed printer width..  In most assembler,=
=20
> the symbol table is sorted, but presented in columns; the order runs from=
=20
> the top of the left-most column down 55 symbols then the next symbol in=
=20
> order is the the right of the first symbol etc.  This was designed to mak=
e=20
> the symbol table more reasonable.  [Most of the number-crunching effort i=
s=20
> the actual table soft.  The trick of sampling the table in multiple place=
s=20
> is a neat minor routine, etc.  When the table is sufficiently wide, the=
=20
> right-hand-most column will extend to approx. column 123, well lower than=
=20
> the 132 column limit.  But this program and font choice does not support =
it.
>
> It is not clear if this is merely a specific interaction and perhaps can=
=20
> be overcome with an alternate font choice, or a bug of just the freeware=
=20
> program [that will always fail regardless of font].  As such, consider th=
is=20
> part of the PEPS package on hold.  [There is no point in documenting how =
to=20
> add a broken component.[
>

=E2=80=8BI'm glad to announce the VeryPDF freeware package has been made to=
 work=20
properly by variation of two of the more obscure parameters passed to the=
=20
program.  The problem is the defaults were inappropriate for =E2=80=8B
=20
=E2=80=8Bthe form size chosen [11 x 17].  Changing to rational non-default =
values=20
has now been confirmed to support the full 132 column width intended, and=
=20
is quite comparable to the support level in the Da Vince packaage.

[Solution took some thinking; the implementation of the fix is more than=20
obvious once you know]=E2=80=8B

